E-Commerce Customer Analytics: SQL → Python → Power BI

An end-to-end data analysis project on the UCI Online Retail dataset (541K+ transactions, Dec 2010–Dec 2011, UK-based online retailer). Covers data cleaning, business analysis, customer segmentation, and an interactive dashboard — using SQL, Python, and Power BI at the stage each tool fits best.

 Project Overview

| Stage | Tool | What it covers |
|---|---|---|
| Data querying & business analysis | SQL (T-SQL) | Aggregation, window functions, RFM scoring, cohort analysis, self-joins |
| Cleaning, modeling & visualization | Python (pandas, matplotlib/seaborn, scikit-learn) | Data cleaning, RFM table, K-Means customer segmentation |
| Interactive dashboard | Power BI | Data model, DAX measures, cross-filtered visuals |

 Key Business Questions Answered
- What's the overall revenue trend, and which months/products/countries drive the business?
- Who are the most valuable customers, and how do we score/rank them (RFM)?
- How well does the business retain customers over time (cohort retention)?
- Can customers be segmented into meaningful groups (Champions, Loyal, At Risk, Lost) using clustering instead of hand-picked rules?
- Which products are frequently bought together?



Highlights & Real Bugs Caught Along the Way
A few genuine debugging moments worth calling out — this project wasn't just following a checklist, it involved catching and fixing real issues:
- SQL:an unintentional cross-join (missing join condition) that was silently comparing every product against every other product instead of matching them correctly
- SQL: an inverted `NTILE` scoring direction in the RFM query — best customers were initially getting the worst scores
- Python: an `ABS()` vs. net revenue distinction that was inflating revenue by treating returns as additional sales
- Power BI: a broken table relationship caused by a date/timestamp mismatch between the fact table and the calendar table, and separately, an inactive relationship silently returning the same total for every month

Customer Segments (from K-Means clustering on RFM)
| Segment | Recency | Frequency | Monetary | Description |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Champions | Very recent | Very high | Very high | Best customers — retain and reward |
| Loyal | Recent | High | High | Strong repeat buyers |
| Occasional | Recent | Low | Low-moderate | Active but infrequent |
| Lost/Hibernating | Long inactive | Very low | Low | Re-engagement campaigns needed |

